{"id":"377e6b6b-5d0b-47ed-9d4d-8b8ee7f1ff7e","object":"chat.completion","created":1784999394,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\":\"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case.get('nodes')\\n    required_symbols = case.get('required_symbols')\\n    if not isinstance(nodes, list) or not isinstance(required_symbols, list):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Invalid case structure\\\")\\n    node_dict = {}\\n    children_of = {}\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        if not isinstance(node, dict):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"Node not a dict\\\")\\n        for key in ('id', 'parent', 'symbol', 'depth', 'removable', 'utility'):\\n            if key not in node:\\n                raise ValueError(f\\\"Node missing field {key}\\\")\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        if not isinstance(nid, str):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"id must be string\\\")\\n        node_dict[nid] = node\\n        children_of.setdefault(nid, [])\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        pid = node['parent']\\n        if pid is not None:\\n            if pid not in node_dict:\\n                raise ValueError(f\\\"Parent {pid} not found\\\")\\n            children_of.setdefault(pid, []).append(node['id'])\\n    root_id = None\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        if node['parent'] is None:\\n            root_id = node['id']\\n            break\\n    if root_id is None:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"No root node\\\")\\n    all_symbols = {node['symbol'] for node in nodes}\\n    for req in required_symbols:\\n        if req not in all_symbols:\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"Required symbol {req} not present\\\")\\n    retained = set(node_dict.keys())\\n    symbol_to_nodes = {}\\n    for nid, node in node_dict.items():\\n        sym = node['symbol']\\n        symbol_to_nodes.setdefault(sym, set()).add(nid)\\n    sorted_ids = sorted(node_dict.keys(), key=lambda nid: (-node_dict[nid]['depth'], nid))\\n    removed_order = []\\n    for nid in sorted_ids:\\n        node = node_dict[nid]\\n        if not node['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        if nid == root_id:\\n            continue\\n        has_retained_child = any(c in retained for c in children_of.get(nid, []))\\n        if has_retained_child:\\n            continue\\n        subtree = set()\\n        stack = [nid]\\n        while stack:\\n            cur = stack.pop()\\n            if cur in subtree:\\n                continue\\n            subtree.add(cur)\\n            for child in children_of.get(cur, []):\\n                stack.append(child)\\n        can_remove = True\\n        for req in required_symbols:\\n            nodes_with_req = symbol_to_nodes.get(req, set())\\n            outside = nodes_with_req - subtree\\n            if not outside:\\n                can_remove = False\\n                break\\n        if not can_remove:\\n            continue\\n        retained -= subtree\\n        removed_order.append(nid)\\n        for subid in subtree:\\n            sym = node_dict[subid]['symbol']\\n            symbol_to_nodes[sym].discard(subid)\\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\\n    return {\\n        \\\"removed_node_ids\\\": removed_order,\\n        \\\"retained_node_ids\\\": retained_sorted\\n    }\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) for task SE-PE-01. The output must be a JSON object with \"removed_node_ids\" (array of strings) and \"retained_node_ids\" (array of strings). The input payload has \"nodes\" (list of node objects) and \"required_symbols\" (list of required symbols). Also a \"_case_nonce\" but not used.\n\nThe problem description: \"grammar_guided_node_reduction\" similar to Perses. We need to remove nodes that are removable, subject to constraints: root not removable, required symbols must remain represented, depth-first (deeper first), stable tie by node ID.\n\nGiven the public fixtures, structure is consistent: nodes have id, parent (null for root), symbol, depth, removable (bool), utility (float). Required_symbols list includes \"root\" and \"s1\". The root node has symbol \"root\". Required_symbols likely include the root symbol and other required symbols that must be present after removal.\n\nWe need to decide which removable nodes to remove. The goal seems to be to minimize tree (reduce) while preserving that for each required symbol, there is at least one node with that symbol in the retained set. Additionally, we must not remove a node that has retained children? The description says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So we must only remove a node if none of its children are retained. That is typical in reduction algorithms: you can only delete a node if you delete its entire subtree, otherwise you'd break the tree.\n\nAlso \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" So before removing a node, we check if after removal (including its entire subtree), all required symbols are still present in the rest of the tree.\n\nOrder: deeper nodes first (higher depth? Actually deeper means larger depth, so process nodes in decreasing depth order). For equal depth, use node ID as tiebreak (string compare? \"n6\" vs \"n4\" etc.)\n\nWe need to return the actual removal order (the order in which nodes were removed) as \"removed_node_ids\", and the remaining nodes in sorted order as \"retained_node_ids\". Sorted probably by node ID (string sort). The description: \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So removed_node_ids should be in the order they were removed (processing order), and retained_node_ids sorted.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Build a dict of nodes by id.\n2. Build parent-child relationships.\n3. Identify root (parent null).\n4. Start with all nodes as retained, except we will try to remove removable nodes.\n5. For each node (sorted by depth descending, then by id ascending for tiebreak), if removable and not root and all required symbols are still represented if we remove it (including its subtree), and it has no retained children (i.e., its subtree is fully removed already or it has no children that are retained), then remove it. Remove means mark it as removed and exclude its subtree from retained set.\n6. But careful: When we remove a node, we remove all its descendants automatically. So we need to maintain a set of retained nodes. Initially all nodes retained. We process nodes in the order described. For each node, if it is removable, we check if all required symbols are still present in the retained set after removing that node and its descendants (i.e., we consider removal of the entire subtree rooted at that node). Also check that the node has no children that are retained (if it has retained children, we cannot remove it unless those children are also being removed at the same time? But the algorithm usually processes depth-first, so children will be processed first. So if we process deeper nodes first, when we consider a node, its children have already been considered for removal. If a child was retained, then the parent cannot be removed. So condition: node has no retained children. That's equivalent to: all its children have been removed (or never existed). Since we process depth descending, children will have been processed before parent. So we can check that the node's current children (the ones in the retained set) are empty. So we need to dynamically update the child list as nodes are removed. Or we can keep a set of removed ids and check that none of its immediate children are in retained set.\n\nSimplify:\n- retained = set(all node ids) initially.\n- For each node in sorted list (depth desc, id asc):\n   if node is removable and node.id != 'root':\n        # Check if node has any retained children (immediate children that are still retained)\n        has_retained_child = any(child_id in retained for child_id in children_of(node.id))\n        if not has_retained_child:\n            # Check if removing this node and its subtree (all descendants) would still have all required symbols\n            # Compute set of symbols lost = symbols of node and all descendants.\n            # If (current required symbols set) minus lost symbols still contains all required, then ok.\n            # Actually we need to check that after removal, the retained set still contains at least one node for each required symbol.\n            # So compute symbols_in_subtree = collect all symbols from node and its descendants.\n            # Then if for each required symbol s, there is at least one node with symbol s in retained \\ subtree_nodes, then proceed.\n            # If so, remove: mark all subtree nodes as removed (remove from retained set), and add node.id to removal_order.\n        else:\n            # cannot remove because children retained\n            continue\n\nBut careful: The condition \"all required symbols remain represented\" applies to the entire tree after removal. So we need to check that. However, note that if we remove a subtree, we lose the symbols in that subtree. So we need to ensure that for each required symbol, there is at least one node not in that subtree that has that symbol.\n\nImplementation details:\n- Build adjacency: parents and children.\n- Precompute descendants for each node? Or compute on the fly using children and recursion.\n- Since tree size is small, we can compute symbol_map: for each symbol, list of node ids with that symbol, initially. Then when removing subtree, we subtract those nodes from symbol_map. But we need to check before removal: if subtree contains all nodes for a particular required symbol, then removal would make that symbol absent. So we need to check for each required symbol whether there exists a node with that symbol outside the subtree.\n- So we can maintain a set of nodes per symbol (symbol_to_nodes). Initially populate. When we remove a subtree, we update symbol_to_nodes accordingly.\n\nProcedure:\n- Parse case: nodes list.\n- Build node_dict: id -> node object (with all fields).\n- Build children: parent_id -> list of child ids.\n- Find root_id: node with parent None.\n- Build list of all node ids (ids).\n- Sort all node ids by depth descending, then by id lexicographically ascending (string compare). depth is integer, id is string.\n- Initialize retained = set(ids)\n- Initialize removed_order = []\n- Initialize symbol_to_nodes: dict symbol -> set of node ids (include all nodes initially).\n- Process each node id in sorted order:\n    node = node_dict[id]\n    if not node['removable']: continue\n    if id == root_id: continue (though root is not removable anyway)\n    # Check if node has retained children\n    children = children_of.get(id, [])\n    has_retained_child = any(c in retained for c in children)\n    if has_retained_child: continue\n    # Compute subtree nodes (node + all descendants)\n    subtree_nodes = set()\n    # BFS/DFS to collect all descendant ids.\n    stack = [id]\n    while stack:\n        cur = stack.pop()\n        if cur in subtree_nodes: continue? but tree, no cycles.\n        subtree_nodes.add(cur)\n        for child in children_of.get(cur, []):\n            stack.append(child)\n    # Now check required symbols:\n    # For each required symbol, is there at least one node with that symbol not in subtree_nodes?\n    can_remove = True\n    for req_sym in required_symbols:\n        nodes_with_sym = symbol_to_nodes.get(req_sym, set())\n        # nodes outside subtree: nodes_with_sym - subtree_nodes\n        if not (nodes_with_sym - subtree_nodes):\n            can_remove = False\n            break\n    if not can_remove: continue\n    # Remove subtree\n    retained -= subtree_nodes\n    removed_order.append(id)\n    # Update symbol_to_nodes: for each node in subtree_nodes, remove from its symbol set\n    for sub_id in subtree_nodes:\n        sym = node_dict[sub_id]['symbol']\n        symbol_to_nodes[sym].discard(sub_id)\n        # if empty set, could delete key but not necessary\n\nAfter processing all nodes, retained set contains ids that were not removed. Sort retained_ids lexicographically (string sort). Return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed_order, \"retained_node_ids\": sorted(retained_ids)}.\n\nNeed to consider that the root is not removable and always retained. Required_symbols include 'root' and 's1' as per fixtures. In the fixture, root always has symbol 'root', and there is at least one node with symbol 's1' (n1 or n4). Ensure correctness.\n\nEdge cases: If the input is invalid (e.g., required symbol missing), there might be no feasible removal? But the specification says \"Raise ValueError for an infeasible or structurally invalid case instead of silently repairing it.\" However, from fixtures, it's always feasible. We can add a check at the beginning: Verify that each required symbol exists in at least one node. If not, raise ValueError.\n\nAlso verify that nodes have required fields: id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. If any missing, raise ValueError.\n\nAlso ensure that the tree is connected? Not needed.\n\nImplementation details: use only standard library, no external dependencies.\n\nNow produce the source code in the required JSON envelope: {\"implementation\": \"...\\ndef solve(case): ...\\n\"}\n\nNeed to escape newlines, quotes, backslashes.\n\nWrite Python code:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    # Extract nodes and required symbols\n    nodes = case.get('nodes')\n    required_symbols = case.get('required_symbols')\n    if not isinstance(nodes, list) or not isinstance(required_symbols, list):\n        raise ValueError(\"Invalid case structure\")\n    # Build node dict and children\n    node_dict = {}\n    children_of = {}\n    for node in nodes:\n        # Check required fields\n        if not isinstance(node, dict):\n            raise ValueError(\"Node not a dict\")\n        for key in ('id', 'parent', 'symbol', 'depth', 'removable', 'utility'):\n            if key not in node:\n                raise ValueError(f\"Node missing field {key}\")\n        nid = node['id']\n        if not isinstance(nid, str):\n            raise ValueError(\"id must be string\")\n        node_dict[nid] = node\n        # Initialize children list\n        children_of.setdefault(nid, [])\n    # Build parent links\n    for node in nodes:\n        pid = node['parent']\n        if pid is not None:\n            if pid not in node_dict:\n                raise ValueError(f\"Parent {pid} not found\")\n            children_of.setdefault(pid, []).append(node['id'])\n    # Find root (parent None)\n    root_id = None\n    for node in nodes:\n        if node['parent'] is None:\n            root_id = node['id']\n            break\n    if root_id is None:\n        raise ValueError(\"No root node\")\n    # Verify required symbols exist\n    all_symbols = {node['symbol'] for node in nodes}\n    for req in required_symbols:\n        if req not in all_symbols:\n            raise ValueError(f\"Required symbol {req} not present\")\n    # Initialize sets\n    retained = set(node_dict.keys())\n    # Symbol to nodes mapping\n    symbol_to_nodes = {}\n    for nid, node in node_dict.items():\n        sym = node['symbol']\n        symbol_to_nodes.setdefault(sym, set()).add(nid)\n    # Sort node ids by depth descending then id ascending\n    sorted_ids = sorted(node_dict.keys(), key=lambda nid: (-node_dict[nid]['depth'], nid))\n    removed_order = []\n    for nid in sorted_ids:\n        node = node_dict[nid]\n        if not node['removable']:\n            continue\n        if nid == root_id:\n            continue\n        # Check if any retained children\n        has_retained_child = any(c in retained for c in children_of.get(nid, []))\n        if has_retained_child:\n            continue\n        # Collect subtree nodes\n        subtree = set()\n        stack = [nid]\n        while stack:\n            cur = stack.pop()\n            if cur in subtree:\n                continue\n            subtree.add(cur)\n            for child in children_of.get(cur, []):\n                stack.append(child)\n        # Check required symbols\n        can_remove = True\n        for req in required_symbols:\n            nodes_with_req = symbol_to_nodes.get(req, set())\n            # Nodes outside subtree\n            outside = nodes_with_req - subtree\n            if not outside:\n                can_remove = False\n                break\n        if not can_remove:\n            continue\n        # Remove subtree\n        retained -= subtree\n        removed_order.append(nid)\n        # Update symbol_to_nodes\n        for subid in subtree:\n            sym = node_dict[subid]['symbol']\n            symbol_to_nodes[sym].discard(subid)\n    # Sort retained ids\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\n    return {\n        \"removed_node_ids\": removed_order,\n        \"retained_node_ids\": retained_sorted\n    }\n\nNote: The public fixture shows nodes with depth and utility but we don't use utility. That's fine.\n\nNow create the JSON envelope.\n\nThe response must be exactly a JSON object with key \"implementation\" and value being the Python source as a string, properly escaped.\n\nI'll write the code and then produce the JSON.\n\nMake sure to escape: newlines -> \\n, backslashes -> \\\\, double quotes -> \\\"\n\nWrite the source as a multiline string with triple quotes? But we need to embed it in JSON, so we must escape everything inside the JSON string. Easiest is to write the Python code as a string in our mind, then use replace (or just write it raw and then JSON encode). I'll manually encode.\n\nI'll write the code as a plain string, then escape.\n\nBut careful: The code contains double quotes, newlines, etc. I'll produce the JSON by writing the code and then using a JSON serializer in my mind. Since I'm an AI, I can generate the correct JSON.\n\nLet me write the full JSON.\n\nThe response must be:\n\n{\"implementation\":\"<python source escaped>\"}\n\nI'll produce that."},"logpr